```
**Day 204 — the `## Productivity` reader's durable record, written into THIS file rather than `CLAUDE.md`, which is where #917/#912's evaluators pointed for a reason that went stale on 2026-09-15.** Both receipts are the same surface and one note discharges them: the code landed, the reader-facing record did not. `/tokens`-style legend: **the classifier is `classify_productivity(day_claims, days_with_task_commits, task_commit_count=None)` and it returns FIVE values, none folded** — `grep -n "^PRODUCTIVITY_[A-Z_]* = " scripts/extract_trajectory.py` prints `2565 PRODUCTIVITY_OK`, `2566 PRODUCTIVITY_IDLE`, `2567 PRODUCTIVITY_NO_CLAIMS`, `2568 PRODUCTIVITY_COULD_NOT_CHECK`, `2583 PRODUCTIVITY_OUT_OF_RANGE`. Three are refusals-or-facts rather than verdicts and the last two are the pair this task exists to keep apart: **`PRODUCTIVITY_COULD_NOT_CHECK` means the slice was EMPTY — nothing to compare against** (the anti-vacuous branch, asserted first), while **`PRODUCTIVITY_OUT_OF_RANGE` means the slice was NON-EMPTY and simply did not span the claiming day.** Two facts, two sentences, two remedies; folding them would put a missing input in the same bucket as a measured one. `PRODUCTIVITY_NO_CLAIMS` is likewise not the alarm — a window where nothing was attempted has nothing missing from it.

**THE MECHANISM, in the words a reader greps for: a day-label artifact makes a real idle day and a phantom one render IDENTICALLY.** The artifact is that there are **two day-clocks and they disagree at and around `midnight`**: the session directory stamp is written when the audit is *pushed* (Day 195's 23:04 session is `day-195-...T232116Z`) while a commit subject's `(HH:MM)` is `SESSION_TIME` from the session's *start*, so a session that runs across `midnight` claims under one day number and files its commits under another. Measured instance: `classify_productivity({195: 5}, {196}, 4)` — claims on `day-195`, commits labelled `day-196` — which is the fixture at `scripts/extract_trajectory.py`'s self-tests and the shape the Day-197 eval-fix commit `0452b8ad` recorded as *"day-195, commits labelled day-196 -- was written as an IDLE artifact"*. Add the harness checkout being **shallow** (50 commits at the time, oldest = day-196's own wrap-up), and `git log --since='14 days ago'` never binds: `days_with_task_commits` was `{197}`, NON-EMPTY, so the anti-vacuous branch correctly did not fire and day-196 fell through to IDLE — *"could not look"* reported as *"looked and found nothing"*. The span test is **inclusive on both sides** (`lo <= day <= hi`), and a day NEWER than `max(observed)` is out of range too, the conservative direction.

**`observed_label_clause(observed_days, observed_commits)` is the evidence half, and it has TWO SHAPES that are never folded into one sentence**: commits exist under *other* labels → the clause NAMES THE SPAN (`day-183..day-197 (14 distinct)`, so a reader can see how far back the window reaches — a window carrying only `day-196` cannot prove `day-195` idle, while one carrying `day-183..day-197` can); no task commits at all → the clause says `there are NO task commits in this window at all` rather than naming a **phantom** day. Reachability is stated rather than implied and the empty shape is a FLOOR, NOT A LIVE BRANCH: routed through `classify_productivity` it resolves to `COULD_NOT_CHECK` and never reaches an IDLE line, because the anti-vacuous branch fires first; it is kept so a hand-built `Productivity` or a future classifier change cannot emit an alarm naming a day that does not exist, and calling it coverage would be the over-claim this reader exists to avoid.

**THE STATED LIMIT, IN THE NEGATIVE: this reader is SELF-DIAGNOSING — it reports which labels the window carried so a reader can see the artifact — and it does NOT reconcile the two day-clocks.** It does not deepen the clone (`counterfactual_green.py` owns `--deepen` for exactly that reason and this reader has no equivalent, deliberately), it does not map a push-time session stamp onto a start-time commit label, and an out-of-range day stays **UNMEASURED** rather than becoming measured. The render carries four numbered limits and limit 1 leads: **IT IS A READER, NOT A GATE** — nothing fails, reverts or files an issue when a day reads idle, so whether it changes anything depends on a planner reading it.

**Verified, not asserted.** `python3 scripts/extract_trajectory.py --test` prints `ALL PASSED`, exit 0 (re-run 2026-09-20, same session as this note). The production join is one line and reuses what was already collected — `claims_by_day(outcomes)` against `{day for day, _ in tasks}` — with **no second git call and no cargo** (#832: a nested cargo rebuilds over the shared `target/debug/yoyo` uplift path and reddened CI for three sessions); the join is on **DAY NUMBERS, never timestamps**, because those two stamps are exactly the pair that disagrees.

**Superseded claim, recorded rather than erased.** #917's task text described the day-196 observation as *"probably a false alarm"*, and that reading is what the fix falsified: the IDLE line was not a false alarm about productivity, it was a **true statement wearing the wrong verdict** — the day really was outside the slice, and the defect was that the reader had no word for that. The superseded phrasing is deliberately **not** reproduced as live text anywhere in the reader (it was never in the code, only in the task plan), so the record of it belongs here; what replaced it is the two-verdict split above. *That* is the reason a grep for `probably a false alarm` returns 0 in both `CLAUDE.md` and this file — the absence of the old wording is not the absence of the claim.
